      en: 'Rising from the Meghna estuary south of Hatiya in Noakhali, Nijhum Dwip is one of the most remote and beautiful islands in Bangladesh. Its keora and gewa forest, planted by the Forest Department on newly formed chars, and its web of creeks were declared a national park in 2001. A handful of spotted deer are said to have been released here in the 1970s; today their descendants roam the forest in herds.\n\nWait quietly at the forest edge at dawn or late afternoon and the deer often appear. In winter thousands of migratory birds settle on the creeks and mudflats, and from the beach near Namar Bazar you can watch the sun rise and set over a vast expanse of water.\n\nLife here moves with the tides: fishing villages, buffalo herds and racks of drying fish show the island’s simple character. Electricity and facilities are limited, but that very stillness is what people come for.',
